QB.push(
{id:1536,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A developer copies an older code sample and appends a date suffix to a current Claude model ID (for example turning the documented ID into `<model-id>-20260401`) before deploying. What is wrong with this?",
o:["A. Current model IDs are dateless, and each one is already a pinned snapshot; appending a date names a model that does not exist and the request is rejected","B. Date suffixes are mandatory in production and optional only in development","C. The suffix is stripped server-side, so the request behaves identically","D. A `-latest` suffix should be used instead so the model auto-upgrades on each release"],a:0,
e:"**Current Claude model IDs are dateless and each ID is itself a pinned snapshot.** There is no `-latest` alias and no date-suffixed variant to append. Sending an unknown ID gets a `400 invalid_request_error`, not a helpful fallback. **Currency note:** dated IDs are a legacy convention your training data may still show; treat any `-YYYYMMDD` suffix in sample code as stale. The transferable practice is to keep the model ID in configuration, not scattered through code, so a deliberate migration is one edit.",
w:"B — there is no such distinction; the ID is the ID in every environment. C — unknown model strings are rejected, not normalised. D — no `-latest` alias exists, and auto-upgrading behind your back is exactly what pinned snapshots are designed to prevent."},

{id:1537,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A regression suite asserts byte-identical completions from a pinned model ID for 200 fixed prompts. It passed for weeks, then began failing on a handful of prompts with no change to the application, the prompt, or the model ID. What is the most likely explanation?",
o:["A. The pinned ID silently rolled forward to a newer snapshot","B. Model weights are fixed per ID, but the serving infrastructure around them can change; pinning buys behavioural stability, not bit-identical output","C. `temperature` must be set to 0 to make output deterministic","D. The model's context window was resized, changing tokenisation"],a:1,
e:"**Pinning a model ID freezes the weights, not the whole serving stack.** Batching, kernels, and numerics on the inference path can change, so identical inputs may produce slightly different token choices over time. The guarantee you get is stability of *capability and behaviour*, not reproducibility of bytes. The transferable principle: assert on properties (schema validity, required facts present, a scored rubric) not on exact strings. **Currency note:** `temperature` is deprecated and returns a 400 on current models, and even where supported, 0 was never fully deterministic.",
w:"A — a pinned dateless ID does not roll forward; that is the point of pinning. C — `temperature` is rejected with a 400 on current models, and at 0 output was still not guaranteed identical. D — a window change would not alter token-by-token sampling on prompts far inside the limit."},

{id:1538,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A pinned snapshot your production service depends on appears on the deprecation schedule with a retirement date nine months out. You have 40 prompts and an effort setting tuned against it, plus a held-out eval. What is the right migration plan?",
o:["A. Wait until close to the retirement date, then swap the ID in configuration and ship, since the API surface is unchanged","B. Freeze on the retiring ID and request an extension so nothing has to be re-tuned","C. Run the existing held-out eval against candidate successors now, re-tune prompts and `output_config.effort` on the winner, and keep the ID in configuration so the production swap is a single change","D. Point the service at a `-latest` alias so future retirements are absorbed automatically"],a:2,
e:"**Retirement is a scheduled event, so treat migration as planned work with the eval as the gate.** A newer snapshot can shift behaviour even with an unchanged request shape: prompts written for the old model are often over-prescriptive, and the right effort level frequently changes between generations. Running your own held-out eval against candidates early converts a deadline into a measured choice. The transferable principle: your eval is the migration test, and a single configuration point for the model ID is what makes the cutover and the rollback cheap.",
w:"A — the API surface being stable says nothing about behaviour; a late swap ships an unvalidated model into production. B — retirement schedules are not negotiated per customer, and it defers the same work under worse time pressure. D — no `-latest` alias exists, and silent auto-upgrade would remove exactly the control you need."},

{id:1539,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A summarisation endpoint on the mid tier must return within 4 s at p95 and currently takes 11 s. The team's plan is to move to the smallest tier. Profiling shows responses average 1,800 output tokens, `output_config.effort` is at its default, and time to first token is under 400 ms.",
o:["A. Only a tier change can help, because generation speed is a fixed property of the tier","B. Raise `max_tokens` so the model stops re-planning its answer","C. Move the work to the Batch API to take it off the request path","D. Tighten the output contract to a short structured summary and lower `output_config.effort`; end-to-end latency here is dominated by tokens generated, and both levers cut tokens without changing tier"],a:3,
e:"**When time to first token is small and total latency is large, you are latency-bound on output length, not on tier.** Wall-clock time scales with tokens generated, and reasoning tokens count. Lowering `output_config.effort` from its default reduces reasoning depth, and a tight output contract (schema, explicit length limit) reduces the visible response. Measure both against the eval before touching tier, because a tier change also changes quality, cache namespace, and migration path. Transferable principle: fix the dominant term first.",
w:"A — per-token speed differs by tier, but the number of tokens is under your control and is the larger term here. B — a higher `max_tokens` only raises the cap; it does not shorten generation and cannot reduce latency. C — batch is asynchronous with a completion window of up to 24 hours, so it cannot serve a 4 s interactive SLO."},

{id:1540,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A team wants a hard ceiling on reasoning tokens and sends `thinking: {\"type\": \"enabled\", \"budget_tokens\": 6000}` on a current model. The request returns a 400. What is the current mechanism for controlling reasoning depth?",
o:["A. Fixed thinking budgets have been removed on current models; depth is tuned with `output_config.effort` (`low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, `max`) over adaptive thinking, with `max_tokens` as the response cap","B. `budget_tokens` is valid but must be at least 1024","C. The request needs an `anthropic-beta` header to enable extended thinking","D. `budget_tokens` is valid but must be strictly less than `max_tokens`"],a:0,
e:"**Currency note:** `thinking: {type: \"enabled\", budget_tokens: N}` is removed on current models and returns a 400. Thinking is now adaptive — the model decides when and how deeply to reason — and you steer the depth/spend trade-off with `output_config.effort`, which is nested inside `output_config` rather than being a top-level parameter. On some models thinking cannot be disabled at all. The two distractors about minimum size and ordering describe genuine constraints on *older* models, which is exactly why they read as plausible.",
w:"B — the 1024 minimum was a real constraint on older models, but the parameter itself no longer exists on current ones. C — extended thinking was never gated behind a beta header, and no header revives a removed parameter. D — the ordering rule also applied to older models only; the parameter is gone, not misconfigured."},

{id:1541,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A two-tier cascade sends everything to the cheap tier first and escalates when the cheap model's self-reported confidence score is below 0.8. An audit finds escalation firing on many trivially easy items, while the wrong answers that cost the most to remediate came back with self-reported confidence of 0.95. What is the fix?",
o:["A. Raise the escalation threshold to 0.95 so more traffic escalates","B. A model's self-reported confidence is not calibrated against its actual error rate, so it cannot gate a cascade; replace it with a signal computed outside the model, such as schema or validator failure, a grounding check against the retrieved source, or disagreement between two independent cheap runs","C. Ask the model to justify its confidence number before emitting it, so the score becomes more accurate","D. Escalate a fixed random 20% of traffic and accept the residual error"],a:1,
e:"**A cascade is only as good as its escalation signal, and self-reported confidence is not a reliable one.** The number is generated text, not a measured probability; a fluent, wrong answer is exactly the case that attracts a high score. A usable signal must be computable by deterministic code without trusting the model's self-assessment: schema validation, a citation-grounding check, a cheap verifier model, or run-to-run disagreement. Transferable principle: **the model proposes, deterministic code decides** — that applies to routing exactly as it does to authorisation.",
w:"A — moving an uncalibrated threshold changes the escalation volume, not its correlation with actual errors; you escalate more and still miss the confident failures. C — asking for a justification produces a more elaborate self-assessment, not a calibrated one, and adds output tokens. D — a random sample catches errors only in proportion to its rate and does nothing to target the hard items."},

{id:1542,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team has four production routes and cost pressure to move one of them to the smallest tier. Which route is the strongest candidate for the smallest tier to close the quality gap on a held-out eval?",
o:["A. Multi-hop reasoning over a 300-page contract, where the answer depends on resolving cross-references between clauses","B. A 25-tool agentic loop that plans and executes a data migration end to end","C. Assigning one of 14 fixed labels to a short support message against an explicit written rubric","D. Free-form judgement calls on ambiguous policy questions where reviewers themselves disagree"],a:2,
e:"**Small models close the gap on narrow, well-specified decisions with short inputs and a closed output space.** A fixed label set plus an explicit rubric removes almost all the judgement the larger tiers are paying for; a handful of few-shot examples pins the remaining ambiguity. The properties that predict the gap *stays open* are long-horizon tool use (per-step error compounds), long-range reasoning over large inputs, and genuinely ambiguous judgement. Confirm on a held-out eval rather than on intuition — but pick the candidate route by these structural properties first.",
w:"A — long-range multi-hop reasoning over a large document is where tier capability differences are widest. B — per-step accuracy compounds multiplicatively over 25 tool calls, so a small per-step gap becomes a large end-to-end gap. D — when human reviewers disagree the task has no crisp target, and the cheaper tier's variance shows up as visible inconsistency."},

{id:1543,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Why is a public benchmark leaderboard a weak basis for choosing a model tier for a specific production task?",
o:["A. Benchmarks are run with a different tokenizer, so the scores are not comparable across tiers","B. Leaderboard results are only refreshed annually, so they are always out of date","C. Benchmark results cannot be reproduced outside the organisation that published them","D. Benchmarks measure aggregate performance on tasks, prompts, and inputs that are not yours; the deciding evidence is a held-out eval built from your own traffic distribution and scored against your own failure costs"],a:3,
e:"**A benchmark is a proxy for a distribution that is not your distribution.** Two tiers three points apart on a public suite can be indistinguishable on your task, or the ordering can invert — because your inputs, prompt, tool surface, and definition of \"wrong\" all differ. Leaderboards are useful for shortlisting candidates; they cannot substitute for measurement. Transferable principle: **shortlist on benchmarks, decide on your own held-out eval**, and score it with the cost of each failure mode rather than plain accuracy.",
w:"A — tokenizer differences change token counts and therefore cost, but they are not why a benchmark score fails to predict your task. B — publication cadence is not the issue; a same-day score on someone else's task still would not decide yours. C — reproducibility is a real concern for some suites, but the fundamental problem is distribution mismatch, which persists even for perfectly reproducible benchmarks."},

{id:1544,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An internal gateway must reject requests that would exceed the target model's context window before forwarding them. The team hardcoded a lookup table of window sizes, which has already drifted out of date twice. What is the correct approach?",
o:["A. Read the limits from the Models API (`GET /v1/models/{id}`), which exposes `max_input_tokens` for the context window and `max_tokens` for the output cap, and cache them per model","B. Read the `context_window` field returned on every Messages API response and cache it","C. Call `/v1/messages/count_tokens`, which returns an error when the content exceeds the target model's window","D. Assume 1M tokens for every model, since all current tiers share the same window"],a:0,
e:"**The Models API is the live source for per-model limits.** Each model object carries `max_input_tokens` (the context window), `max_tokens` (the output ceiling), and a `capabilities` block — note there is no field literally named `context_window`. Fetching and caching these removes the hardcoded table that drifts on every release. Remember the window bounds request **plus** generated output including thinking tokens, so a gateway check must budget for the response, not just the prompt.",
w:"B — no `context_window` field exists on the Messages response; the limits live on the Models API. C — `count_tokens` returns exactly one field, `input_tokens`; it is an estimate and does not validate against a model's window. D — window sizes differ by tier: the flagship tiers are around 1M while the smallest tier is 200K, so a flat assumption fails in both directions."},

{id:1545,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A cheaper tier costs roughly a quarter of the incumbent per request. In a shadow run on real traffic it needs 2.4 tool-loop turns per task against the incumbent's 1.1, and 18% of its tasks are retried after a validator failure against the incumbent's 3%. How should the team decide?",
o:["A. The cheaper tier still wins, because per-token price is the only quantity that scales with volume","B. Compare cost per *completed* task: multiply the per-request cost by the observed turns and by the rework rate; a 4x per-request price gap can narrow sharply or invert once loop length and retries are priced in","C. Split traffic 50/50 permanently so the average cost lands between the two","D. Move the cheaper tier to the Batch API so the discount offsets the extra turns"],a:1,
e:"**Judge cost per completed task, not cost per request.** A request that needs more loop iterations re-sends the whole accumulated history each time (the API is stateless), and a task that gets retried pays for its input twice. Here the cheaper tier's effective multiplier is roughly 2.4 turns and about 1.18 attempts against 1.1 and 1.03 — enough to consume most of the headline gap before you count the latency and the validator's own cost. Transferable principle: normalise every cost comparison to the unit of work the business cares about.",
w:"A — per-token price is only one factor of the product; turns and retries multiply it. C — an arbitrary permanent split averages two costs without answering which is cheaper per completed task, and doubles the operational surface. D — batch is asynchronous with a completion window of up to 24 hours, so it does not apply to an interactive tool loop, and halving the price of extra turns does not remove them."},

{id:1546,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Before building a two-model cost cascade with a routing layer, what is the single most valuable thing to measure first?",
o:["A. The public benchmark gap between the two candidate tiers","B. The p99 latency of the cheaper tier under load","C. The current model at a lower `output_config.effort` on the same held-out eval — lower effort on a newer model often matches a previous generation at high effort, and one model keeps one cache namespace, whereas a cascade splits caches across models","D. The 429 rate of the cheaper tier at the expected escalation volume"],a:2,
e:"**Try the cheap version of the simple design before you build the complex one.** Effort is a within-model cost lever: dropping from the default to `medium` or `low` can recover most of a cascade's savings with no router, no escalation signal, and no second model to evaluate and migrate. It also preserves cache locality, because **prompt caches are model-scoped** — a cascade forfeits reuse across its models and pays a fresh cache write on every escalation. Transferable principle: measure the one-model baseline before adding architecture.",
w:"A — a benchmark gap does not tell you whether the cheaper design is viable on your task, and it says nothing about the effort lever. B — latency matters for the SLO but is not what decides whether the cascade is needed at all. D — throttling is a capacity concern to plan for later; it does not answer whether a second model earns its complexity."},

{id:1547,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent runs about 30 tool-calling steps per task. On the cheap tier it gets 96% of individual steps right, but only 31% of tasks complete correctly end to end. The team suspects the eval is broken. What is actually happening, and what moves task success?",
o:["A. The eval is measuring the wrong unit; per-step accuracy is the meaningful metric for an agent","B. `max_tokens` is truncating the final step, so the task fails at the last hop","C. Retry the whole task three times; three attempts at 31% gives acceptable coverage","D. Per-step accuracy compounds multiplicatively over a long horizon, so 0.96 raised to the 30th power is about 0.29 — matching what is observed; a stronger tier or higher effort on the decision steps, plus verification that catches a bad step before it propagates, is what moves end-to-end success"],a:3,
e:"**Long-horizon agentic work is where tier differences are amplified, because per-step errors compound.** At 30 steps, a 96% step accuracy yields roughly 29% task success; raising step accuracy to 99.5% yields about 86%. This is why the Opus tier's stated intent is complex agentic and enterprise work: the interesting number is not the per-call price but the per-task success rate. The other half of the fix is structural — a verifier or checkpoint that stops a wrong step from being built on reduces the effective horizon.",
w:"A — per-step accuracy is a diagnostic, not the outcome; the user experiences whole tasks, and the eval is correctly measuring those. B — truncation would show as `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` on a specific step, not as a compounding pattern that matches the arithmetic. C — retries help only if failures are independent and cheap to detect, and three attempts at 31% still leaves roughly a third of tasks failing while tripling cost."},

{id:1548,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which workload most justifies the tier that sits above Opus in Claude's model lineup?",
o:["A. The most demanding reasoning and long-horizon autonomous work, where correctness dominates token price and cheaper tiers have measurably plateaued on your own eval","B. Any request whose input exceeds 200K tokens","C. Any workload that needs streaming responses","D. High-volume classification, because the extra capability amortises over the request count"],a:0,
e:"**Tier intent runs Haiku (fastest and cheapest) → Sonnet (balanced speed and intelligence) → Opus (complex agentic and enterprise) → a higher tier above Opus for the most demanding reasoning.** The top tier earns its price only where the task is genuinely reasoning-bound and cheaper tiers have stopped improving on your measurements. The qualifier matters: the justification is a plateau observed on your own held-out eval, not the belief that harder problems deserve a bigger model.",
w:"B — window size is a separate axis; several tiers carry large windows, so input length alone does not select the top tier. C — streaming is a delivery option on the Messages API and is available across tiers. D — high-volume classification is the canonical case for the *cheapest* tier; volume multiplies the price gap rather than amortising it."},

{id:1549,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"After `output_config.effort: \"max\"` produced a clear win on a coding-agent route, a team raises effort to `max` globally. The chat route and the ticket-classification route get slower and noticeably more expensive, with flat eval scores. What is the correct reading?",
o:["A. Effort only affects agentic tool-calling routes, so the chat and classification changes must have another cause","B. Effort trades thoroughness for token spend and repays it only on reasoning-bound work; coding and long-horizon agentic tasks respond strongly, chat and classification usually do not, so effort should be tuned per route rather than set globally","C. Roll back to the default and treat effort as a parameter that should never be changed","D. Raising effort requires a matching increase in `max_tokens` before the quality gain appears"],a:1,
e:"**Effort is a per-route lever, not a global quality dial.** It controls reasoning depth and overall token spend, so raising it always costs more; whether that buys anything is a property of the workload. Coding and long-horizon agentic work respond to deeper reasoning; classification against a fixed rubric and short conversational turns typically do not, and often hold quality at `low` or `medium`. Transferable principle: tune cost levers at the granularity where the workload varies, and let the eval for each route decide.",
w:"A — effort applies to any request on a supporting model, which is precisely why the non-agentic routes got slower and pricier. C — the default is a reasonable starting point, not an untouchable one; effort is one of the most useful cost and quality levers available. D — `max_tokens` is only an output cap; raising it does not unlock a quality gain and lowering it does not suppress one."},

{id:1550,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"To cut cost, a team sends `thinking: {\"type\": \"disabled\"}` across two routes. On one model the request returns a 400. On the other it succeeds, but the responses start containing visible step-by-step reasoning and occasionally a described tool call that never executes. What is the cost-safe approach?",
o:["A. Retry the 400 with an `anthropic-beta` header that permits disabling thinking","B. Set `max_tokens` low enough that the reasoning is cut off before it reaches the user","C. On some current models thinking cannot be disabled at all, and where it can be, turning it off can push reasoning into the visible response and cause tool calls to be described in text rather than emitted as `tool_use` blocks; keep adaptive thinking on and lower `output_config.effort` instead","D. Move both routes to a smaller tier, where thinking is not available and the problem cannot occur"],a:2,
e:"**Disabling thinking is not the cost lever it looks like.** On some current models it is rejected outright; where it is accepted, the reasoning does not disappear — it can surface as visible prose, and in an agentic loop a tool call written into text simply never runs, with no error raised. Keeping adaptive thinking on and lowering `output_config.effort` reduces reasoning tokens *and* keeps the structured `tool_use` path intact. Transferable principle: reduce the amount of reasoning, do not try to switch reasoning off.",
w:"A — no beta header re-enables a configuration the model does not support; the 400 is a capability statement. B — truncating with `max_tokens` produces `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` and a half-finished answer, and you are still billed for every token generated. D — a tier change to dodge a parameter is a large quality decision made for the wrong reason, and it does not address the described-tool-call failure."},

{id:1551,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"Traffic on a single document-QA route is bimodal: about 85% of requests are one-line factual lookups and 15% require reasoning across several sections. The route currently sends everything to the strong tier. What is the best routing design?",
o:["A. Keep one tier for all traffic; a single model is always cheaper to operate than a router","B. Send every request to the strong tier first and ask it whether the cheap tier could have handled it, then cache that decision","C. Send everything to the cheap tier and let users re-ask when the answer is poor","D. Route up front on cheap, measurable input features (question length, number of documents in scope, presence of comparative or multi-clause phrasing), with thresholds fitted on a held-out set and the strong tier as the default when the router is unsure"],a:3,
e:"**Routing on observable input features is the cheap, reliable form of tier selection.** The features are computed by your code before any model call, so the decision costs nothing and is auditable; fitting thresholds on a held-out set makes the accuracy/cost trade-off explicit; and defaulting to the strong tier on uncertainty puts the failure mode on the safe side. This is the workflow pattern **routing** — a predefined code path — rather than letting the model direct its own process.",
w:"A — a single tier for bimodal traffic overpays on 85% of requests; operational simplicity is a real cost but not an automatic winner at that ratio. B — asking the strong model whether the cheap one would suffice pays full price for the very call you were trying to avoid, and the answer is an uncalibrated self-assessment. C — pushing the failure onto users degrades the product and often costs more, because the re-ask is a second full request."},

{id:1552,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team iterated prompts across 30 attempts while scoring on the same 40 labelled examples. The cheap tier now scores level with the strong tier, so they ship the cheap tier. Production quality regresses within a week. What went wrong, and what is the fix?",
o:["A. The prompts were tuned on the measurement set, so the score is an in-sample fit rather than a generalisation estimate; split into a train set and a held-out set, tune on train, decide on held-out, and size the held-out set so the tier gap exceeds its confidence interval","B. Forty examples is sufficient if they are stratified by category, so the eval was sound and the cheap tier degraded after release","C. The strong tier's advantage only appears at production volume, which no offline eval can capture","D. The comparison needed `temperature: 0` on both tiers to remove sampling noise"],a:0,
e:"**Tuning and measuring on the same examples turns an eval into a fitting exercise.** Thirty prompt iterations against 40 fixed items is enough to encode those items' quirks, and a two-tier gap of a few points is well inside the sampling error of 40 samples anyway. The fix is a train/held-out split with the decision made only on held-out data, and a held-out set large enough that the observed gap is bigger than its confidence interval.",
w:"B — stratification helps representativeness but does nothing about the in-sample fitting or the tiny sample size. C — offline evals built from real traffic predict production quality well; the failure here is methodological, not a volume effect. D — `temperature` is deprecated and returns a 400 on current models, and output was never fully deterministic at 0 in any case."},

{id:1553,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"Two tiers score 94.0% and 98.5% on a held-out eval. The output is posted automatically into customers' accounting ledgers with no human review, and reversing an incorrect posting requires a manually issued credit note. Volume is 150,000 postings a month. What should decide the tier?",
o:["A. Choose the cheaper tier and add a blanket retry, since retries are cheaper than the price gap","B. Compute expected cost: error rate x remediation cost x volume, and compare it with the token-price delta. At this volume a 4.5-point error gap is 6,750 extra manual credit notes a month, which can dwarf the price difference; and a deterministic validation gate before posting is what makes either tier safe","C. Choose the cheaper tier and disclose the error rate to customers in the terms of service","D. Choose the stronger tier and skip the validation gate, since 98.5% is high enough for an unreviewed pipeline"],a:1,
e:"**When failures are unreviewed and expensive to reverse, the token-price delta is usually the smallest term in the decision.** 4.5 points of 150,000 is 6,750 remediations a month; price that against the per-request delta and the answer is usually arithmetic, not taste. The second half matters just as much: **the model is not a security or correctness boundary**, so a deterministic validator in front of the ledger write is required whichever tier wins — it converts silent bad postings into caught, retryable failures.",
w:"A — a retry only helps when the failure is detected, and with no review and no validator nothing detects it. C — disclosure changes your legal exposure, not the 6,750 credit notes or the operational cost of issuing them. D — a 1.5% error rate at 150,000 postings is still over 2,000 bad ledger entries a month; a stronger tier reduces the rate but never removes the need for the gate."},

{id:1554,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team standardises on a particular tier and builds six months of prompts and evals against it. A new enterprise customer then requires the workload to run inside their own cloud account. What is the lesson?",
o:["A. Point the first-party SDK client at the cloud provider's endpoint with a `base_url` override and keep the same model","B. Any first-party model can be reached from any cloud by adding a provider header to the request","C. Model and feature availability differs across first-party, Amazon Bedrock, Google Cloud, and Microsoft Foundry, so the deployment constraint must be checked before the tier is fixed — a platform requirement can eliminate a tier or a feature outright","D. Bedrock and Google Cloud serve an identical model and feature set to the first-party API, so deployment target never constrains tier choice"],a:2,
e:"**Deployment platform is a hard constraint on model selection, not a deployment detail to settle later.** Claude is available first-party and on Amazon Bedrock, Google Cloud, and Microsoft Foundry, but the model set and the per-feature availability are not identical across them — as a concrete example, on Bedrock and Google Cloud only `base64` image sources are available. Elicit the deployment target during requirements gathering, and keep the model ID plus any platform-specific code paths behind one configuration boundary.",
w:"A — the SDKs expose dedicated provider clients for each platform; a `base_url` override on the first-party client does not supply the platform's auth or model naming, and it does not create availability that is not there. B — no header makes a model available on a platform that does not serve it. D — the sets overlap but are not identical, and per-feature availability differs even for models that are present on both."},

{id:1555,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A pipeline uses the cheap tier both to draft customer emails and, in a second call, to grade its own drafts against a rubric. The automated pass rate is 97%, but human sampling finds 22% of shipped emails defective. Where should the model budget move?",
o:["A. Add a more detailed rubric to the grading prompt and keep both roles on the cheap tier","B. Have the grader score each draft twice and average the two scores","C. Have the drafting call grade itself in the same response, saving one request","D. Judging is usually harder than producing: put the stronger tier or a higher effort setting on the grader, and calibrate the grader against human labels before its pass rate is trusted as a control"],a:3,
e:"**In an evaluator-optimiser arrangement, the judge is the component that must be at least as capable as the worker.** A weak grader produces a pass rate that looks like quality assurance while measuring almost nothing, which is worse than no gate because it suppresses the signal. Two moves fix it: raise the judge's tier or effort, and calibrate the judge against human labels so its pass rate has a known relationship to the defect rate. Transferable principle: spend capability where an error is invisible, not where it is obvious.",
w:"A — a sharper rubric helps a capable grader; it does not give a weak grader the discrimination it lacks, and it leaves the 97%/78% gap unexplained. B — averaging two draws from the same weak grader reduces variance around a biased estimate; it does not move the bias. C — self-grading inside the same response is the weakest possible check, since the model is scoring text it has just committed to."},

{id:1556,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A long-running agent reliably fills its context window around turn 60. The proposal on the table is to move from the 200K-window tier to a roughly 1M-window tier. What is the strongest objection?",
o:["A. A bigger window only postpones the wall and makes every later turn more expensive, because the API is stateless and the whole accumulated history is re-sent and billed on each request; the structural fixes are compaction, clearing stale tool results, and delegating reading-heavy work to a subagent that returns a summary","B. The larger window is billed at a premium per-token rate above 200K, so the move is uneconomic","C. Window size cannot be the deciding factor in model choice under any circumstances","D. Turn count, not token count, is what exhausts the window, so a larger window changes nothing"],a:0,
e:"**Context growth in an agent is a cost problem before it is a capacity problem.** Statelessness means turn 60 re-sends everything from turns 1 to 59, so a 5x window buys roughly 5x the turns at steadily rising per-turn cost. Compaction summarises earlier context, context editing clears stale tool results, and a subagent with its own window returns a short finding instead of the documents it read. A larger window is a legitimate answer when a single indivisible input demands it — but not as a substitute for context management.",
w:"B — the full window on current models is billed at standard per-token rates; there is no long-context surcharge, so this objection is factually wrong. C — window size genuinely decides when one indivisible input exceeds a tier's limit; the objection here is that this is not that case. D — the window is measured in tokens; turns matter only through the tokens they accumulate."},

{id:1557,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which statement about `output_config.effort` on models that support it is correct?",
o:["A. It is a top-level `effort` parameter taking an integer from 1 to 10","B. It accepts `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, and `max`, is set inside `output_config`, and omitting it applies the model's default level","C. It is a boolean that turns extended thinking on or off","D. It replaces `max_tokens` as the cap on response length"],a:1,
e:"**Effort is an ordered enum nested inside `output_config`:** `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, `max`. It steers reasoning depth and overall token spend over adaptive thinking, and omitting the field applies the model's default. **Currency note:** effort replaces the removed fixed-budget mechanism (`thinking: {type: \"enabled\", budget_tokens: N}`, now a 400 on current models) and is generally available with no beta header. `max_tokens` remains a separate hard cap on the response.",
w:"A — it is a named level inside `output_config`, not a top-level numeric scale. C — it is not a toggle; on some models thinking cannot be disabled at all, and effort tunes depth rather than switching reasoning off. D — `max_tokens` is unchanged and still required; effort influences how many tokens the model chooses to spend, not the ceiling."},

{id:1558,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An internal reporting tool makes about 300 model requests a month. An engineer proposes two sprints of work to build a router and a caching layer that would cut its token bill by 60%. How should the team respond?",
o:["A. Approve it; a 60% reduction is always worth capturing because savings compound with future growth","B. Approve it but scope it to caching only, since caching is free of quality trade-offs","C. At 300 requests a month the absolute token spend is negligible next to two sprints of engineering time, so use the strongest tier that meets the latency requirement and revisit only when volume makes the bill material","D. Reject it and move the tool to the cheapest tier instead, capturing most of the saving for no engineering effort"],a:2,
e:"**Cost optimisation is a function of absolute spend, not of percentage savings.** Sixty per cent of a trivial bill is still trivial, while two sprints is a real, immediate cost — and every lever added is code to maintain, debug, and migrate. At this volume the right default is the strongest tier that meets the latency SLO, with a volume threshold agreed in advance that triggers a revisit. Transferable principle: size the optimisation against the bill it addresses, and let engineering time appear in the comparison.",
w:"A — percentage savings on a negligible base do not justify sprints, and speculative future growth is not a reason to build now. B — caching being quality-neutral is true but irrelevant; the objection is the size of the prize, not the type of lever. D — down-tiering is cheap to do but is a real quality decision that still needs an eval, and at this volume it buys almost nothing while risking output quality."},

{id:1559,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A held-out eval of 500 items shows two tiers tied within noise, so the team picks the cheaper 200K-window tier. In production a small fraction of requests fail intermittently with `stop_reason: \"model_context_window_exceeded\"` or a 400. Investigation shows those requests carry 300K-400K token documents. What is the real error?",
o:["A. The tie was correct and the failures are a rate-limit problem that retry logic will absorb","B. `max_tokens` was set too high, consuming window that the input needed","C. The tie should have been broken on latency rather than on price","D. The eval set never contained a long document, so the tie was an artefact of an unrepresentative sample; the long tail of inputs exceeds the cheaper tier's 200K window, and an eval must cover the tail before a tie between tiers means anything"],a:3,
e:"**A tie only licenses a choice over the distribution the eval actually sampled.** Here the eval was drawn from typical traffic and silently excluded the tail that carries the binding constraint — window size. Note the two distinct symptoms: an input that alone exceeds the window is a request-time 400, whereas overflow during generation surfaces as `stop_reason: \"model_context_window_exceeded\"`, because the window bounds request plus generated output including thinking. Transferable principle: build the eval from the distribution including its tail, then break ties on the constraint that binds.",
w:"A — a rate-limit failure is a 429 with `retry-after`, and it does not correlate with document size; retrying a request that cannot fit reproduces the same failure. B — trimming `max_tokens` cannot make a 350K input fit a 200K window, and `max_tokens` above the model's ceiling is rejected rather than clamped. C — latency is a reasonable tie-break in general, but choosing on latency here would have picked the same failing tier."},

{id:1560,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A grounded question-answering route is moved from the default effort level to `max`. Accuracy improves by 1 point while cost rises 2.7x. Error analysis of the remaining failures shows about 80% are questions whose answer was not present in any retrieved passage. What should the team do?",
o:["A. Revert the effort increase and spend the budget on retrieval quality — chunking, reranking, and coverage — because no amount of reasoning depth can recover information that never entered the context","B. Keep `max` effort and also raise the retrieval top-k, so both bottlenecks are addressed at once","C. Move to a stronger tier, since effort has clearly reached its ceiling on this task","D. Enlarge the eval set, because a 1-point movement is not statistically detectable"],a:0,
e:"**Deeper reasoning cannot fix a missing-evidence problem.** When the dominant failure mode is that the answer was never retrieved, the task is retrieval-bound, and effort — which trades tokens for thoroughness — buys a rounding error at 2.7x the price. Diagnose the failure mode first, then apply the lever that addresses it. Transferable principle: raising reasoning depth is worth its cost only on reasoning-bound work; profile the errors before choosing the lever.",
w:"B — raising top-k on top of `max` effort compounds two costs and adds distractor passages, which is a cost and a quality problem at once. C — a stronger tier reasons better over the same context, so it cannot supply the missing passage either. D — statistical power is a fair general concern, but the error analysis has already identified the cause; a bigger eval would only measure the same 1-point gain more precisely."},

{id:1561,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"For a new feature of uncertain feasibility, which sequence of work minimises wasted effort in selecting a model tier?",
o:["A. Start on the cheapest tier and move up only when users complain, so you never overpay","B. Build on a strong tier until the task is provably solvable and a held-out eval exists, then down-tier route by route with that eval as the gate — this separates \"is this task feasible at all\" from \"how cheaply can it be done\"","C. Benchmark all available tiers on synthetic examples before writing the first prompt","D. Build two full implementations in parallel, one cheap and one strong, and compare them in production"],a:1,
e:"**Establish feasibility first, then optimise cost against a fixed quality bar.** Starting cheap conflates two failures — the task may be impossible, or the tier may be too weak — and you cannot tell which you are looking at. Proving the task out on a strong tier produces the artefact you need for every later decision: a held-out eval with a known achievable score. Down-tiering then becomes a measurable, reversible step per route. Transferable principle: **make it work, then make it cheap**, with the eval as the ratchet that stops cost work from silently costing quality.",
w:"A — user complaints are a slow, expensive, and biased quality signal, and you cannot distinguish an infeasible task from an under-powered tier. C — synthetic examples do not represent your distribution, and benchmarking before a prompt exists measures the wrong thing entirely. D — two full implementations doubles the build and maintenance cost to answer a question a single eval answers offline."},

{id:1562,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which is a legitimate reason to prefer a smaller model tier even when budget is not a constraint?",
o:["A. Smaller tiers hallucinate less, because they have less latitude to invent content","B. Smaller context windows keep the model focused and improve accuracy on long inputs","C. Smaller tiers generate tokens faster, so a strict interactive latency requirement can select them on speed alone","D. Smaller tiers produce more deterministic output, making them easier to test"],a:2,
e:"**Latency is an independent selection axis from cost.** The Haiku tier's stated intent is fastest and cheapest; where a user-facing SLO is tight, the speed half of that can decide the choice even with budget to spare — provided a held-out eval confirms the quality is adequate for the task. The disciplined framing is: enumerate the binding constraints (latency, window, platform availability, error cost), then choose the cheapest tier that satisfies all of them and clears the eval bar.",
w:"A — hallucination is not monotonic in tier, and smaller models are generally *more* prone to confident fabrication, not less. B — a small window is a limitation, not a focusing device; degradation on long context is a separate effect that a small window does not cure. D — no tier is fully deterministic; output varies run to run regardless of size, so testing must assert on properties rather than exact strings."},

{id:1563,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A cascade sends every request to the cheap tier and escalates to the strong tier on a verifier signal. The strong tier costs about 5x the cheap tier per request. In production the escalation rate settles at 70%, and the team reports the cascade is \"saving money\". How should this be assessed?",
o:["A. The cascade saves 30% of the bill, matching the share of traffic that stays on the cheap tier","B. The cascade is losing money, because every escalated request pays for two model calls","C. The cascade breaks even at any escalation rate below 100%, so 70% is fine","D. Total cost is roughly (1 x cheap) + (0.70 x 5 x cheap) = 4.5 against 5.0 for strong-only — about a 10% saving, bought by adding a full extra round trip to 70% of requests plus the verifier's own cost; either the escalation signal must become far more selective or requests should be routed up front on input features"],a:3,
e:"**A cascade's economics live entirely in its escalation rate, and the break-even point is higher than intuition suggests.** With a 5x price ratio the cheap first pass is pure overhead on escalated traffic, so the saving is `1 - (1 + r x 5) / 5`: about 70% at r = 0.1, 30% at r = 0.4, and only 10% at r = 0.7. Add the latency of a second round trip and the loss of cache locality (**caches are model-scoped**) and a high-escalation cascade is often worse than routing up front. Compute the break-even before building one.",
w:"A — the saving is not the share of traffic retained; the escalated 70% pays the cheap call as well as the strong one. B — it does pay twice on escalated requests, but the arithmetic still lands slightly under strong-only, so \"losing money\" overstates it; the real objection is that a 10% saving does not pay for the complexity and latency. C — break-even is well below 100%: the cheap pass adds its own cost, so the cascade is worse than strong-only once the escalation rate exceeds 4/5 at this price ratio."},

{id:1564,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A cost-reduction mandate says \"move every route to the cheapest tier that passes its eval\". One route resists: a 25-tool code-migration agent whose task completion drops from 78% to 19% on the cheapest tier. How should the mandate be applied?",
o:["A. Cost optimisation is decided per route, and on this route the cheapest tier is more expensive per completed task, not less; exempt it with the eval evidence recorded, and continue down-tiering the routes where the eval holds","B. Cut the agent's tool count until the cheapest tier can cope with it","C. Keep the cheapest tier and retry each failed task three times to recover the completion rate","D. Decompose the agent into 25 independent single-tool calls so each is simple enough for the cheapest tier"],a:0,
e:"**A blanket tier mandate picks the wrong unit of decision.** Cost per completed task is what matters, and a route whose completion rate falls by three quarters burns more tokens per finished job even at a quarter of the per-token price — before counting the human cleanup. The right shape for the mandate is a per-route decision with the held-out eval as the gate, plus an explicit written exemption so the decision is revisited when a new tier ships rather than relitigated informally.",
w:"B — the tool surface should be consolidated on its own merits, but shrinking it to fit a tier trades away the agent's capability to satisfy a budget rule. C — retries help only when failures are independent and detectable; three attempts at 19% still leaves roughly half of tasks failing and triples the token spend. D — the agent's value is in sequencing and adapting across tools, which a fan-out of independent single-tool calls cannot reproduce."},

{id:1565,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"On a held-out eval, the mid tier at `max` effort and the strong tier at its default effort both reach the target score. Mid-at-max costs 1.9x mid-at-default; strong-at-default costs 2.5x mid-at-default. Mid-at-max is measurably slower at p95 than strong-at-default. Which decision procedure is sound?",
o:["A. Always prefer the stronger tier; effort is a stopgap that will be undone by the next model release","B. Neither option dominates, so decide on the constraint that actually binds: price the p95 latency requirement, then weigh operational factors — one model means one cache namespace, one migration path, and one prompt to maintain — and pick accordingly rather than on the cost ratio alone","C. Always prefer the cheaper tier at higher effort, because within-model levers are free of migration risk","D. Ship both and split traffic randomly so the average cost and latency land between the options"],a:1,
e:"**When two configurations tie on quality but differ on price and latency in opposite directions, no rule picks a winner — the binding constraint does.** Put a number on the latency requirement (what does a p95 breach cost?) and add the operational terms: prompt caches are model-scoped, so running two models splits cache reuse, and each model carries its own migration and deprecation work. The transferable principle: when a comparison has no dominant option, make the trade-off explicit and decide it against a stated requirement.",
w:"A — a stronger tier at the default is not automatically right; here it is 32% more expensive for the same eval score, and effort is a generally available lever, not a stopgap. C — within-model levers avoid a migration but are not free: `max` effort is the slowest setting, and the latency requirement may rule it out. D — a random split leaves you owning both configurations and their maintenance, and averages two costs instead of satisfying a requirement."},

{id:1566,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A legal-review tool must reason over a single 480,000-token court filing, where any chunking destroys the cross-references between exhibits that the analysis depends on. Two candidate tiers score similarly on the team's eval; one has a 200K window and the other around 1M. What decides?",
o:["A. Chunk the filing anyway and reconcile the chunk outputs with a map-reduce pass","B. Retrieve the 20 most relevant passages and analyse those","C. This is the case where window size genuinely decides: the input is indivisible and no chunking or retrieval preserves the cross-references, so only a tier whose window exceeds the input plus the generated output including thinking tokens is viable, and the 200K tier is eliminated regardless of price or eval score","D. Either tier works, because the API automatically pages inputs that exceed the window"],a:2,
e:"**Window size is the deciding factor when a single indivisible input will not fit — and rarely otherwise.** The test is structural: if chunking or retrieval preserves the reasoning, the window is a cost and engineering question; if it destroys the reasoning, the window is a hard constraint. Budget for the whole request: the window bounds input **plus generated output including thinking tokens**, so 480K of input plus a long analysis needs real headroom, not a window that merely exceeds the input.",
w:"A — map-reduce over chunks is the right tool when chunks are independent; here the cross-exhibit references are precisely what chunking severs. B — top-k retrieval discards the document structure the analysis depends on and cannot know in advance which exhibits a cross-reference will reach. D — there is no automatic paging; an over-window request is rejected, and overflow during generation surfaces as `stop_reason: \"model_context_window_exceeded\"`."},

{id:1567,d:"D2",s:"Model Selection & Trade-offs",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A team avoids sending large prompts to a roughly 1M-token-window model because they assume long context carries a surcharge. What is actually true?",
o:["A. Requests above 200K tokens are billed at a premium input rate on every tier","B. Prompt caching and the batch discount are unavailable above 200K tokens","C. Requests above 200K tokens require an `anthropic-beta` header and are billed separately","D. On current models the full window is billed at standard per-token rates — a 900K-token request costs the same per token as a 9K one — and caching and batch discounts apply across the whole window; the real cost of a long prompt is that you pay for every one of those tokens on every stateless request"],a:3,
e:"**There is no long-context surcharge on current models; the cost of long context is volume, not rate.** That reframes the problem: a 900K prefix is expensive because a stateless API re-sends and re-bills it on every call, so the levers are prompt caching on the stable part (reads at 0.1x base input), tighter retrieval, and context management — not avoidance of the window. Transferable principle: separate *rate* from *quantity* when reasoning about cost, because they have completely different remedies.",
w:"A — the full window is priced at standard rates on current models; no volume-based rate tier exists. B — both discounts apply across the full context window; caching in particular is the main defence against a large stable prefix. C — the large window is generally available on current models with no beta header and no separate billing line."},

{id:1568,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team is given a mandate to cut Claude spend by 40%. Their first action is to switch the busiest route to a cheaper tier. What should have come first?",
o:["A. Read the `usage` block from a representative sample of real production requests and compute the token profile — `input_tokens`, `output_tokens`, `cache_creation_input_tokens`, `cache_read_input_tokens` — per route, because the profile determines which levers can move the bill at all","B. Enable the Batch API on every route to capture the discount immediately","C. Lower `max_tokens` globally, since output is the more expensive direction","D. Open a volume-discount negotiation, since engineering levers are secondary at scale"],a:0,
e:"**Measure the token profile before choosing a lever.** `usage` on every response gives you the split, and the split decides everything: if input is 95% of tokens, tightening output contracts is noise; if a large stable prefix dominates, caching is the single biggest lever; if history accumulation dominates, compaction is. Switching tier first trades quality for money without knowing whether money was on that axis. Transferable principle: **profile, then apply the free levers (caching, prompt hygiene, batch) before the levers that trade quality (tier, effort).**",
w:"B — batch gives roughly 50% off but only suits work tolerant of a completion window up to 24 hours; applied blindly it breaks interactive routes. C — you are billed for tokens generated, not for `max_tokens`, so lowering the cap saves nothing unless it was binding, and it truncates responses when it is. D — commercial terms may help eventually, but they do not tell you where the tokens are going, and they are not an engineering lever."},

{id:1569,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A support assistant averages 38,000 input tokens and 260 output tokens per request. On its tier, output tokens are priced 5x input tokens. Where is the spend, and what follows?",
o:["A. Output, because it is priced 5x higher; shortening replies is the highest-leverage change","B. Input, by roughly 29:1 — the 5x output multiplier is swamped by a 146:1 token-volume ratio, so caching, prompt trimming, and tighter retrieval are the levers that matter and shortening replies is noise","C. Roughly even, so both directions should be worked in parallel","D. Neither; the injected tool-use system prompt dominates at this scale"],a:1,
e:"**Output is priced above input, but input usually dominates the bill by volume.** In cost units: input 38,000 x 1 = 38,000; output 260 x 5 = 1,300 — input is about 97% of the total and roughly 29x the output cost. This is the standard shape for retrieval- and instruction-heavy applications, and it is why the highest-leverage levers are almost always input-side: prompt caching on the stable prefix, trimming accumulated instructions and few-shot examples, and retrieving less.",
w:"A — the 5x rate is real but applies to 260 tokens against 38,000; halving reply length would cut the bill by under 2%. C — a 29:1 ratio is not even, and working both in parallel spends engineering effort where it cannot pay back. D — the tool-use system prompt is a few hundred tokens, real but negligible against 38,000 input tokens."},

{id:1570,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A cost dashboard computes spend by summing `usage.input_tokens` and multiplying by the input rate. After prompt caching is enabled, reported input tokens fall by about 90% but the invoice barely moves. What is wrong with the dashboard?",
o:["A. Cache reads are free, so the dashboard is right and the invoice must include unrelated charges","B. Cache writes are billed as output tokens, which the dashboard is attributing to the wrong side","C. `usage.input_tokens` counts only the tokens that were neither read from nor written to cache; total input is `input_tokens + cache_creation_input_tokens + cache_read_input_tokens`, and the three price differently — writes at 1.25x base input for the 5-minute TTL (2x for 1 hour) and reads at 0.1x. The dashboard is silently dropping the two largest buckets","D. The batch discount is being applied to the invoice but not to the dashboard"],a:2,
e:"**The three input fields in `usage` are mutually exclusive, not nested.** `input_tokens` is defined as the tokens neither read from nor used to create a cache, so enabling caching moves most of the volume into `cache_read_input_tokens` and makes a naive dashboard look like a 90% win. Correct accounting sums all three and applies each multiplier: 1x, 1.25x (or 2x at the 1-hour TTL), and 0.1x. Transferable principle: any cost or attribution report must enumerate every billed `usage` field, not just the familiar two.",
w:"A — cache reads are heavily discounted at 0.1x base input, not free, and they are reported in their own field. B — cache writes are billed as input tokens at a 1.25x (or 2x) multiplier; output pricing is unrelated. D — batch is a separate discount that would make the invoice *lower* than the dashboard, which is the opposite of the symptom."},

{id:1571,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A 60,000-request batch job puts a 30,000-token shared instruction block behind a `cache_control` breakpoint using the default TTL. The results show `cache_creation_input_tokens` populated on a large fraction of requests and `cache_read_input_tokens` on the rest. What is happening and what is the fix?",
o:["A. Batch requests cannot use prompt caching at all, so every request is charged as a fresh write","B. Because batch results are returned in no guaranteed order, cache reuse within a batch is inherently random","C. Each request needs its own `custom_id` for the cache to key correctly across the batch","D. The default cache TTL is 5 minutes and only refreshes on a read, but a batch may take up to 24 hours to complete, so entries expire between processing waves and are rewritten at 1.25x base input. Use the 1-hour TTL for batch work; the ~50% batch discount and the cache multipliers stack"],a:3,
e:"**The default 5-minute cache TTL and the Batch API's 24-hour completion window are mismatched.** The TTL refreshes on each read, so a steady interactive stream keeps an entry warm — but batch processing is scheduled in waves with arbitrary gaps, so entries lapse and are rewritten at the 1.25x write multiplier instead of being read at 0.1x. Selecting the 1-hour TTL costs 2x on writes but survives the gaps. The two discounts compose: batch halves the rate and caching reduces the volume charged at full rate.",
w:"A — caching works with batch requests; the problem is TTL expiry, not incompatibility. B — result ordering affects how you key your own results (always by `custom_id`), not cache hits, which depend on prefix identity and TTL. C — `custom_id` is required on every batch request for result correlation, but it plays no part in cache keying, which is byte-identity of the prefix."},

{id:1572,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"To cut cost, a team lowers `max_tokens` from 8192 to 512 across all routes. The monthly bill is unchanged, 4% of responses now truncate with `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`, and the 429 rate on output tokens per minute is also unchanged. Why did nothing improve?",
o:["A. You are billed for tokens actually generated, not for `max_tokens`; responses already averaged around 300 tokens so the cap was never binding, and `max_tokens` does not factor into output-token rate limits either. Reducing output cost requires changing what the model produces — a tight output contract, a schema, an explicit length limit","B. The cap should have been lowered to 256; 512 is still above the effective threshold","C. Billing is computed from `max_tokens` but applied on the following invoice cycle, so the saving has not appeared yet","D. Truncation proves the cap is now binding, so the saving will follow once the retry logic settles"],a:0,
e:"**`max_tokens` is a safety ceiling, not a cost lever or a rate-limit lever.** Billing follows the tokens generated, so a cap above the natural response length changes nothing; a cap below it truncates and forces a retry, which costs *more* because the input is paid for twice. The documented rate-limit dimensions are requests per minute and input and output tokens per minute, and `max_tokens` does not enter the output-token calculation — lowering it relieves no throttling. Control output cost with the output contract and set `max_tokens` from the measured p99.",
w:"B — the cap is not the mechanism at all; a lower number only truncates more responses. C — there is no lagged `max_tokens`-based billing; usage is metered on actual tokens. D — truncation is a defect, not progress: each truncated attempt is billed in full and the retry pays for the whole input again."},

{id:1573,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A report generator emits about 2,400 output tokens per call, of which roughly 900 are a preamble, a restatement of the request, and a closing summary that the downstream consumer discards. What most directly reduces output cost?",
o:["A. Lower `max_tokens` to 1,500 so the discarded tail is cut off","B. Constrain the response with `output_config.format` set to `{\"type\": \"json_schema\", ...}` so constrained decoding emits only the fields the consumer reads; the discarded prose is never generated, which is the only way output tokens actually fall","C. Prefill the assistant turn with an opening brace so the model skips straight to the payload","D. Keep the prompt as-is and strip the preamble in post-processing, so the consumer sees only what it needs"],a:1,
e:"**The only way to stop paying for output tokens is to stop generating them.** Native structured output via `output_config.format` with a `json_schema` uses **constrained decoding**, so the response is schema-valid by construction and there is no room for preamble or sign-off. That removes roughly 40% of the output volume here and also removes the parsing fragility. The tool-side equivalent is `strict: true` on a tool definition. **Currency note:** this supersedes both the schema-bearing-tool workaround and the assistant-prefill trick.",
w:"A — a lower cap truncates mid-sentence with `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`; you are still billed for everything generated and now have a broken response. C — assistant prefill returns a 400 on current models; the brace trick is legacy and must never be recommended. D — post-processing discards tokens you have already paid for, so the bill is unchanged."},

{id:1574,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"How are tool definitions billed on the Messages API?",
o:["A. Once per conversation, after which they are cached automatically at no charge","B. Only on requests where Claude actually emits a `tool_use` block","C. As input tokens on every request — names, descriptions, and JSON Schemas — plus a fixed tool-use system prompt that the API injects whenever any tool is present; a forced `tool_choice` of `any` or `tool` makes that injected prompt somewhat larger than `auto` or `none`","D. As output tokens, because they shape what the model generates"],a:2,
e:"**Tool definitions are input tokens on every single request, because the API is stateless.** The `tools` array is re-sent and re-billed each call, and whenever at least one tool is present the API also injects a tool-use system prompt whose size varies by model and by `tool_choice`. That is why a growing tool catalogue raises the bill with no prompt change, and why the standing advice is to **consolidate tools and sharpen descriptions rather than add more**. Caching helps only if you place a breakpoint and the tool list stays byte-identical.",
w:"A — nothing is cached without an explicit `cache_control` breakpoint (or top-level auto-caching), and even then reads are billed at 0.1x, not free. B — the definitions are part of the prompt the model reads in order to decide, so they are billed whether or not a call is emitted. D — they are input, not output; output tokens are only what the model generates."},

{id:1575,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent carries 90 tool definitions costing roughly 48,000 input tokens per request. The team sets `defer_loading: true` on all 90 and adds the tool search tool. What actually happens?",
o:["A. Nothing is saved, because every definition is still transmitted in `tools` on each request","B. Deferred tools are also stripped from the response, so `tool_use` blocks stop being emitted","C. Deferred tools remain in context by name only, so about half the cost is recovered","D. Deferred tools are excluded from the model's context entirely — not even their names — which is where the input-token saving comes from, while you still transmit every definition in `tools` on each request; and at least one tool must have `defer_loading: false`, so deferring all 90 returns a 400"],a:3,
e:"**`defer_loading` separates what you transmit from what the model is billed to read.** Deferred definitions are excluded from the model's context completely, names included, and are surfaced on demand through the tool search tool — that exclusion is the token saving. You still send the full `tools` array on every request (bandwidth, not context), and the request 400s if every tool is deferred, since the search tool itself must remain loaded. Treat it as the exception for genuinely large catalogues; consolidating overlapping tools remains the first move.",
w:"A — transmission and context inclusion are different things; deferred definitions are not put in the model's context, so they are not billed as input tokens. B — deferral affects only what the model sees up front; tools discovered through search are called normally and return `tool_use` blocks. C — the exclusion is total, not name-only, which is precisely why the saving is large."},

{id:1576,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"An agent's per-task cost is dominated by re-sending accumulated tool results on every iteration. Two mechanisms are proposed: context editing and compaction. How do they differ?",
o:["A. Context editing *clears* stale content from the history — old tool results, and optionally the tool inputs or thinking blocks — while compaction *summarises* earlier context when it approaches a threshold. Both shrink the prefix you re-send and are billed for on each turn, but one discards and one condenses","B. They are two names for the same mechanism, differing only in whether it runs client-side or server-side","C. Neither affects cost; both only postpone hitting the context window","D. Compaction clears tool results and context editing summarises them"],a:0,
e:"**Both remedies attack the same cost driver — a stateless API re-bills the whole accumulated history every iteration — but they do it differently.** Context editing removes selected blocks (stale tool results, optionally their inputs, or thinking blocks); compaction replaces earlier context with a model-written summary when the conversation nears a trigger threshold. Clearing is cheaper and lossy in a predictable way; summarising retains gist at the cost of a summarisation call. Both reduce every subsequent turn's bill, not just the current one.",
w:"B — they are distinct features with distinct configuration; conflating them leads teams to expect summaries where content was simply deleted. C — cost is the primary effect: the shrunken prefix is what you pay for on every later turn, and the window relief is a consequence. D — the definitions are the right way round in only one option; this one inverts them."},

{id:1577,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A research agent's context reaches about 400,000 tokens because it reads 30 source documents inline, and every subsequent turn re-sends and re-bills all of it. Which change addresses the structure of the problem?",
o:["A. Cache the whole accumulated history at the 1-hour TTL and accept that it keeps growing","B. Delegate the reading to subagents, each with its own context window, and have each return a short structured finding; the parent's history then grows by summaries rather than by whole documents, so the prefix re-billed on every turn stays small","C. Move to a tier with a larger context window so the documents fit comfortably","D. Split the 30 documents across 30 separate batch requests and reassemble the answers"],a:1,
e:"**Subagents are a context and cost boundary as well as an organisational one.** Each gets its own context window and returns only its conclusion, so the reading cost is paid once inside the subagent instead of being carried in the parent's prefix and re-billed on every remaining turn. This is the orchestrator-workers shape — classified as a **workflow**, since the decomposition follows a predefined code path. Transferable principle: keep the long-lived context small and push bulk reading to short-lived contexts.",
w:"A — caching lowers the rate on re-sent tokens to 0.1x but does not stop unbounded growth; you still pay that rate on 400,000 tokens every turn, plus a write each time the tail changes. C — a larger window postpones the wall while making every later turn more expensive, since the whole history is still re-sent. D — batch changes the price and the latency of the reads, not the fact that the parent context ends up holding all 30 documents."},

{id:1578,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A tool returns a 34,000-token JSON payload from an internal API. The model uses six fields from it. The payload then sits in the conversation history and is re-sent on every subsequent turn. What is the highest-leverage fix?",
o:["A. Instruct the model in the system prompt to ignore the fields it does not need","B. Move to a larger-window tier so the payload is no longer a constraint","C. Project the result inside your tool handler before returning it — return only the fields the model needs, in the shape it needs; the reduction applies to this request and to every later turn that re-sends the history, and removing irrelevant text also improves accuracy","D. Gzip the JSON and return it base64-encoded so it occupies fewer tokens"],a:2,
e:"**Tool-result projection is one of the largest and safest agent cost levers, because savings compound across turns.** Your handler already knows the schema, so filtering to six fields is deterministic code — no model call, no quality risk. It cuts the current request and every subsequent one that re-sends the history through a stateless API. It is also a quality win: irrelevant content competes for the model's attention and can degrade accuracy well inside the window limit. Transferable principle: **shape tool outputs for the model, not for the upstream API's convenience.**",
w:"A — an instruction not to read something still requires the tokens to be sent and billed, and it is advisory rather than enforced. B — a larger window removes the ceiling but not the per-turn cost of re-sending 34,000 tokens. D — compressed base64 is not fewer tokens; binary-looking text tokenises poorly and the model cannot read it at all."},

{id:1579,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Finance needs per-team cost attribution and per-team spend limits on a shared Claude integration. What is the right platform-level mechanism?",
o:["A. Add a `team_id` field to the request body so the platform tags the usage","B. Parse a team tag out of the model's response and aggregate on it","C. Ask each team to self-report its estimated usage monthly","D. Give each team its own workspace and API key so spend is already segregated and limitable at the platform level, and log per-request `usage` keyed by team for granularity finer than the workspace rollup"],a:3,
e:"**Attribution belongs to the credential and the workspace, not to the request body.** Separate API keys inside per-team workspaces give segregated spend, per-workspace limits, and independent rate-limit budgets without any application change. Layer request-level logging on top when you need per-feature or per-customer detail: record the model ID and every billed `usage` field — `input_tokens`, `output_tokens`, `cache_creation_input_tokens`, `cache_read_input_tokens` — since the cache buckets are separate fields with their own multipliers. Note a configured workspace spend limit surfaces as a **400**, while a usage-tier monthly cap surfaces as a **429**.",
w:"A — the Messages API takes no arbitrary attribution field, and an unknown body parameter is a 400 rather than a tag. B — the model's output is not a billing record; it is untrusted text and can be wrong or absent. C — self-reporting is neither accurate nor enforceable, and it gives finance no ability to cap a team's spend."},

{id:1580,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"An organisation has a configured workspace spend limit and is also subject to its usage tier's monthly cap. Which status codes surface each of these when they are hit?",
o:["A. A configured organisation or workspace spend limit returns 400 `invalid_request_error`; reaching the usage-tier monthly cap returns 429","B. Both return 429 `rate_limit_error`","C. Both return 402 `billing_error`","D. The spend limit returns 402 and the usage-tier cap returns 403"],a:0,
e:"**The two caps report differently, and handling code must distinguish them.** A limit you configured yourself is a request-validity problem: **400 `invalid_request_error`**. The usage-tier monthly cap is throttling: **429**. That difference matters operationally — a 429 is retryable and carries `retry-after`, while retrying the 400 just reproduces it until a human raises the limit. 402 `billing_error` exists but signals a billing-account problem, not a hit ceiling. Alerting should therefore branch on the error `type`, not just on the presence of a failure.",
w:"B — only the usage-tier cap is a 429; treating the configured spend limit as retryable produces a hot retry loop against a wall. C — 402 `billing_error` covers billing-account problems, not either of these ceilings. D — 403 `permission_error` concerns authorisation for an operation, and 402 is not what a configured spend limit returns."},

{id:1581,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"Total monthly Claude spend has been flat for three months. A post-incident review finds that a prompt change tripled tokens per completed task while request volume fell by two thirds over the same period. What does this say about the monitoring?",
o:["A. Total spend is the correct signal because it is what the organisation actually pays","B. Total spend conflates volume with efficiency; the alerting signal should be cost (or tokens) per completed task, broken out per route and per `usage` bucket, so an efficiency regression is visible even when the invoice is flat","C. Request count is the signal that would have caught this","D. The 429 rate is the leading indicator that would have caught this"],a:1,
e:"**A flat invoice can hide a threefold efficiency regression when volume moves the other way.** Budget monitoring needs a normalised unit — cost per completed task, or tokens per task — computed per route from the `usage` fields, with alerts on the derived ratio rather than on the aggregate. Break it out by bucket too: a jump in `cache_creation_input_tokens` relative to `cache_read_input_tokens` localises the regression to a broken cache prefix rather than to model behaviour. Keep the absolute-spend alert as a backstop, not as the primary signal.",
w:"A — total spend is what you pay but it is a lagging aggregate; it cannot separate \"more work\" from \"more waste\". C — request count fell, so a volume alert would have shown an apparent improvement rather than a regression. D — the 429 rate reflects throughput against rate limits and is unrelated to tokens consumed per task."},

{id:1582,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"What can `POST /v1/messages/count_tokens` contribute to cost control, and what are its limits?",
o:["A. It returns both the input count and a projected output count, so a full cost estimate can be produced before sending","B. It is billed at 0.1x the input rate, the same as a cache read","C. It is free and returns exactly one field, `input_tokens`, covering messages, system, and tools — so it can gate oversized requests before you pay for them and support pre-flight estimates, but it says nothing about output, is an estimate, is tokenizer-specific, has its own rate limit independent of message creation, and does not exercise prompt caching","D. It requires `max_tokens` and returns a dollar estimate for the request"],a:2,
e:"**`count_tokens` is a free, input-only pre-flight check.** It returns a single field, `input_tokens`, covering messages plus system plus tools, and it is tokenizer-specific — so counts must be re-measured per model rather than carried across a migration. Two operational caveats: it has its own requests-per-minute limit separate from message creation, so a call per request is a throughput consideration; and it does not create or read cache entries, so it cannot be used to pre-warm a cache. Output cost still has to be estimated from measured response lengths.",
w:"A — no output projection is returned; output length is a property of generation and must be measured empirically. B — the endpoint is free; the 0.1x multiplier belongs to prompt-cache reads. D — `max_tokens` is not required, and the endpoint returns token counts, never currency."},

{id:1583,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"Compare two designs for 1,000,000 monthly requests, using cost units where an input token is 1 and an output token is 5.\n\nDesign 1: a 20,000-token uncached prefix, 500 further input tokens, 300 output tokens.\nDesign 2: the same prefix behind a cache breakpoint, achieving a 90% hit rate (misses pay the 1.25x write, hits pay 0.1x).\n\nRoughly what does Design 2 save?",
o:["A. About 90%, matching the cache hit rate","B. Nothing, because cache writes cost more than uncached input","C. About 50%, because the output multiplier limits the achievable saving","D. About 71% — roughly a 3.5x reduction: 22,000 units drops to about 6,300, because the prefix bills at 0.1x on hits and 1.25x on the 10% of misses while the 300 output tokens stay at 1,500 units and were never the problem"],a:3,
e:"**Work the arithmetic in cost units rather than guessing from the multipliers.** Design 1: 20,500 x 1 + 300 x 5 = 22,000. Design 2 prefix: 0.9 x (20,000 x 0.1) + 0.1 x (20,000 x 1.25) = 1,800 + 2,500 = 4,300; plus 500 uncached input and 1,500 output gives about 6,300 — a 71% saving. Two lessons: the saving is capped below the hit rate because writes cost 1.25x and the uncached remainder still bills at 1x, and the irreducible floor is the part you cannot cache.",
w:"A — the hit rate is not the saving; the 10% of misses pay a 1.25x write premium and the uncached input and output tokens are unaffected. B — a 5-minute cache write is 1.25x base input and a read is 0.1x, so the entry repays itself after a single read; at a 90% hit rate caching is strongly positive. C — output is only 1,500 of 22,000 units here, so it constrains almost nothing; the number lands nearer 71% than 50%."},

{id:1584,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A retrieval pipeline fetches whole source documents (averaging 14,000 tokens each) rather than passages, on the reasoning that more context is safer. Input tokens run about 62,000 per query, and answers frequently cite the wrong section of the right document. What should change?",
o:["A. Retrieve at passage granularity with a reranker and a hard per-query token budget: over-retrieval is simultaneously a cost problem (you pay for every token on every stateless request) and a quality problem (irrelevant passages compete for the model's attention and produce exactly this wrong-section failure)","B. Put the retrieved documents behind a cache breakpoint so the repeated cost drops to 0.1x","C. Move to a 1M-window tier so 62,000 tokens is comfortable","D. Raise `output_config.effort` so the model reasons harder about which section is relevant"],a:0,
e:"**Over-retrieval is the rare defect where the cost fix and the quality fix are the same change.** Fewer, better-targeted passages cut input tokens directly and remove the distractors causing the mis-citation — models degrade on long context well inside the window limit. The practical shape is passage-level chunking, a reranker over the candidate set, and a per-query token budget that forces the ranking to matter. Measure it as recall at the passage level, not as \"did we include enough\".",
w:"B — per-query retrieved content differs on every request, so it cannot form a stable byte-identical prefix; you would pay a 1.25x write almost every time and never read. C — a bigger window removes a ceiling you have not hit and does nothing about either the per-query cost or the wrong-section errors. D — deeper reasoning cannot compensate for a context stuffed with near-miss passages, and it adds cost on top of the existing input cost."},

{id:1585,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A request places per-query retrieved passages first in the `messages` array, then 12 stable few-shot examples, then the user's question, with a `cache_control` breakpoint after the examples. `cache_read_input_tokens` is zero on every request. Why?",
o:["A. Few-shot examples in `messages` are not eligible for caching; only `system` and `tools` are","B. The cacheable prefix is rendered `tools` → `system` → `messages` and must be byte-identical up to the breakpoint. Putting per-query passages ahead of the stable examples changes the prefix on every request, so nothing after them can be reused; stable content must come first and volatile content after the last breakpoint","C. The request needs a fifth breakpoint to cover the examples separately","D. Retrieved content is never cacheable, which disqualifies the whole request"],a:1,
e:"**Cache reuse is prefix-based, so one volatile block early poisons everything after it.** The cacheable prefix is assembled `tools` → `system` → `messages` and must match byte for byte; per-query passages placed before the examples guarantee a miss and a 1.25x write on every call. Reorder so the stable material (tools, system, few-shot examples) precedes the breakpoint and the volatile material (retrieved passages, the question, any timestamp) follows it. Transferable principle: **order by volatility, then put the breakpoint at the volatility boundary.**",
w:"A — message blocks are cacheable; the ordering, not the location, is the defect. C — the maximum is 4 breakpoints and a 5th returns a 400; more breakpoints cannot rescue a prefix that changes every request. D — retrieved content can sit inside a cached prefix when it is stable across requests; here it is per-query, which is why it must go after the breakpoint."},

{id:1586,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which workload is the best fit for the Batch API's roughly 50% discount?",
o:["A. An interactive chat assistant that must stream a first token within 500 ms","B. A voice assistant that streams partial transcriptions back to the caller","C. An overnight re-classification of 400,000 archived support tickets, where completion any time within 24 hours is acceptable","D. A synchronous fraud check inside a card-payment authorisation flow"],a:2,
e:"**Batch trades latency for roughly half the token price on both input and output.** Requests complete within a 24-hour window, so the only qualifying workloads are those with no interactive latency requirement — bulk classification, backfills, offline enrichment, eval runs. Operational details that matter: each request needs a `custom_id` because results are JSONL in no guaranteed order, the limits are 100,000 requests or 256 MB (whichever comes first), and a `processing_status` of `\"ended\"` means the batch finished, not that every request succeeded.",
w:"A — a 24-hour completion window cannot serve a 500 ms first-token requirement. B — streaming partial output to a live caller is fundamentally incompatible with asynchronous batch processing. D — a payment authorisation is synchronous and latency-critical, so batch is unavailable to it regardless of the discount."},

{id:1587,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A production prompt carries 24 few-shot examples totalling about 9,000 tokens, accumulated over a year as individual incidents were patched, with no measurement of whether any of them still help. What is the right treatment?",
o:["A. Remove all of them; current models follow written instructions and no longer need examples","B. Put them behind a cache breakpoint and stop worrying about the count","C. Compress them by abbreviating field names and stripping whitespace","D. Ablate them against a held-out eval: remove examples in groups and keep the smallest set that holds the score. Caching lowers the rate on whatever survives, but a shorter stable prefix is cheaper still, and examples that no longer earn their place can also bias the output"],a:3,
e:"**Accumulated few-shot examples are unmeasured cost until you ablate them.** Group-wise removal against a held-out eval gives you a defensible minimum set and often reveals examples that actively skew behaviour toward a stale pattern. Sequence the levers correctly: shrink the prefix first, then cache what remains — caching the survivors at 0.1x is excellent, but caching examples that contribute nothing just makes a useless cost cheaper. Note that any edit to the examples invalidates the cached prefix, so batch the trimming into one change.",
w:"A — examples remain useful for pinning format and edge-case handling; a blanket deletion is as unmeasured as the accumulation was. B — caching is the right second step but it is not a substitute for measurement, and you still pay 0.1x on every token plus a write whenever the prefix changes. C — abbreviating field names saves a small fraction, makes the examples harder to maintain, and can degrade the very format-pinning they exist for."},

{id:1588,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent's tool catalogue has grown to 41 tools with substantially overlapping responsibilities. Input tokens per request are up 3x year on year and the model selects the wrong tool on about 15% of turns. What is the right move?",
o:["A. Consolidate the overlapping tools and sharpen the remaining descriptions: fewer, well-described tools cut the per-request input bill and raise selection accuracy at the same time, whereas adding another tool to disambiguate makes both worse","B. Add a router tool whose job is to pick which of the 41 tools to use","C. Set `disable_parallel_tool_use` so the model commits to one tool per turn","D. Force `tool_choice: {\"type\": \"any\"}` so the model must always call something"],a:0,
e:"**A bloated tool surface is a cost problem and an accuracy problem with one shared fix.** Definitions are re-billed as input tokens on every stateless request, and overlapping descriptions make the selection decision genuinely ambiguous — so consolidation pays twice. The documented guidance is to **improve descriptions and consolidate before adding tools**. Where a catalogue is irreducibly large, `defer_loading` plus the tool search tool keeps deferred definitions out of context entirely, but that is the exception after consolidation, not instead of it.",
w:"B — a router tool adds a 42nd definition to the bill and moves the ambiguity rather than removing it. C — `disable_parallel_tool_use` limits how many calls are made per turn; it does nothing about which tool is chosen or what the definitions cost. D — a forced `tool_choice` of `any` also enlarges the injected tool-use system prompt, and compelling a call cannot make an ambiguous choice correct."},

{id:1589,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"How are thinking (reasoning) tokens accounted for in billing and in the context window?",
o:["A. They are not billed, since they are internal to the model and not returned to the caller","B. They are billed as output tokens, and they consume the same context window as the request and the visible response — the window bounds the request plus generated output including thinking. That is why `output_config.effort` is a direct cost lever, and why a long reasoning trace on an already-large request can end in `stop_reason: \"model_context_window_exceeded\"`","C. They are billed at 0.1x base input, the same multiplier as a cache read","D. They are billed as input tokens on the following turn, when they are replayed in the history"],a:1,
e:"**Thinking tokens are output tokens for both billing and window accounting.** The context window bounds request plus generated output *including* thinking, so reasoning is not a separate budget — it competes with the visible response for the same space, and overflow during generation surfaces as `stop_reason: \"model_context_window_exceeded\"`. Because output is priced above input, reasoning depth is one of the few output-side levers that genuinely moves the bill, which is exactly what `output_config.effort` tunes. Display settings change visibility only; the tokens are generated and billed either way.",
w:"A — they are generated tokens and are billed as such; visibility of the reasoning is a separate setting from whether it happened. C — 0.1x is the prompt-cache read multiplier and has nothing to do with reasoning. D — replayed thinking blocks are part of the history you resend, but the reasoning is billed as output on the turn that produced it."},

{id:1590,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A document pipeline sends page screenshots at 2,400 x 3,100 pixels. Input tokens per page come in far higher than the team expected from their text-based estimates. What explains it and what is the lever?",
o:["A. Images are billed per file at a flat rate that is higher than text, so the count is not comparable","B. The images should be converted to PNG, which tokenises more efficiently than JPEG","C. Visual tokens are counted in 28 x 28 patches — ceil(w/28) x ceil(h/28), about 9,500 tokens for this page — so the count scales with pixel area; downscaling to the lowest resolution at which the text stays legible is the direct lever, and referencing the image by Files API `file_id` avoids resending the bytes on every turn of a multi-turn conversation","D. Lowering `max_tokens` will bring the per-page cost down"],a:2,
e:"**Image cost is a function of pixel area, not file size or format.** The current formula counts visual tokens in 28 x 28 patches: `ceil(w/28) x ceil(h/28)`, so 2,400 x 3,100 is 86 x 111 ≈ 9,546 tokens for one page. **Currency note:** the older `(w x h)/750` approximation is gone. Downscaling is therefore the primary lever — halving each dimension cuts tokens roughly fourfold. In multi-turn use, a Files API `file_id` source avoids re-transmitting base64 bytes each turn, since the API is stateless.",
w:"A — there is no flat per-file image charge; images are billed as input tokens derived from their dimensions. B — the supported formats (JPEG, PNG, GIF, WebP) all resolve to the same patch-based token count; encoding affects transfer size, not tokens. D — `max_tokens` caps the response and has no bearing on input image cost."},

{id:1591,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"About 9% of report generations end with `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`. The service detects this and retries the request from scratch with a higher cap. What is the cost consequence and the right fix?",
o:["A. Truncated attempts are not billed, since the response was unusable, so only the latency matters","B. Lower `max_tokens` further so truncation happens earlier and less is wasted","C. Move the route to the Batch API so the retries are half price","D. Every truncated attempt is billed in full for the input it consumed and the output it produced before the cap, so a retry from scratch pays for the whole input twice. Set `max_tokens` from the measured p99 output length rather than optimistically low, and tighten the output contract so that p99 falls"],a:3,
e:"**A truncation-and-retry loop is a pure cost multiplier on the input side.** The first attempt bills its full input plus everything generated up to the cap; the retry bills the whole input again. At 9% truncation on an input-dominated request, that is a material and entirely avoidable surcharge. The fix is empirical: measure the output-length distribution, set `max_tokens` above the p99, and reduce the distribution itself with a schema or an explicit length limit — rather than treating the cap as a budget control, which it is not.",
w:"A — usage is metered on tokens processed and generated; a truncated response is billed. B — an earlier cap increases the truncation rate and therefore the number of double-billed retries. C — batch halves the rate on a workload that tolerates a 24-hour window, but it does not remove the retries and does not apply to an interactive route."},

{id:1592,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A team enables `\"stream\": true` on their busiest route hoping to reduce cost. What is the effect on the bill?",
o:["A. None: streaming changes how the same tokens are delivered, not how many are generated or billed. Its benefits are a much better time to first token and avoiding request timeouts on long outputs","B. Output token cost is halved, because tokens are billed on delivery rather than on generation","C. Input tokens are billed once per stream rather than per request, which reduces cost on multi-turn conversations","D. Streaming adds a 1.25x surcharge on output tokens, the same multiplier as a cache write"],a:0,
e:"**Streaming is a delivery mechanism, not a pricing mechanism.** The same input is processed and the same output is generated, so `usage` and the invoice are unchanged; you simply receive `content_block_delta` events as tokens are produced. Its real value is perceived latency and avoiding HTTP timeouts on long generations — which is why large `max_tokens` values effectively require it. Remember that the final `stop_reason` and the cumulative output usage arrive in **`message_delta`**, not `message_stop`, so cost accounting must read that event.",
w:"B — billing follows tokens generated regardless of transport; there is no delivery-based discount. C — the API is stateless and every request is billed for its full input, streaming or not. D — the 1.25x multiplier belongs to 5-minute prompt-cache writes; streaming carries no surcharge."},

{id:1593,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A system prompt has grown to 6,200 tokens, roughly half of it accumulated boilerplate — \"be careful\", \"do not hallucinate\", \"think step by step\" — added after individual incidents with no measurement. It is stable across requests. What is the right treatment?",
o:["A. Cache it behind a breakpoint and leave the content alone, since a stable prefix caches perfectly","B. Ablate it against a held-out eval: remove the accumulated boilerplate in blocks and keep only what measurably changes the score. A stable prompt should be cached as well, but caching a prompt that does nothing merely makes a useless cost cheaper, and long low-signal instructions dilute the ones that matter","C. Move the boilerplate into the first `user` message so it falls outside the system prompt","D. Split the prompt across 5 cache breakpoints so each section can be invalidated independently"],a:1,
e:"**Caching and pruning are complementary, and pruning comes first.** Caching reduces the *rate* on a stable prefix to 0.1x; pruning reduces the *quantity*, which compounds with caching and also improves quality, since a wall of low-signal instructions competes with the directives that actually matter. Ablate in blocks against a held-out eval so each removal is evidence-backed, then place the breakpoint on the surviving prefix. Note that every edit invalidates the cached prefix, so batch the trimming into a single change rather than dribbling it out.",
w:"A — this captures the rate saving but leaves 3,000 tokens of unmeasured instruction in place, still billed at 0.1x on every request and still diluting the useful directives. C — moving instructions into a `user` message weakens their authority and changes nothing about the token count; the top-level `system` parameter is the correct place for operator instructions. D — the maximum is 4 breakpoints and a 5th returns a 400, and a stable prompt needs one breakpoint, not several."},

{id:1594,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A cost-capping wrapper sets `max_tokens` to the same large constant for every model it fronts. Requests to one particular model begin returning 400s. What is going on, and what else is wrong with the design?",
o:["A. The value is silently clamped to each model's ceiling, so the 400 must have another cause","B. This is the overflow condition and would surface as `stop_reason: \"model_context_window_exceeded\"` rather than a 400","C. A `max_tokens` above a model's output ceiling is rejected with a 400 rather than clamped, and the ceiling differs per model, so a per-model value discoverable from the Models API is required. Separately, `max_tokens` is not a cost cap at all — you are billed for tokens generated — and it does not relieve output-token rate limits either","D. The `anthropic-version` header is missing, which surfaces as a 400 only on some models"],a:2,
e:"**`max_tokens` above a model's output ceiling is a validation error, not a value to be clamped.** Ceilings differ per model, so a shared constant across a fleet is guaranteed to break on the model with the lowest one; read `max_tokens` from the Models API per model instead. The deeper design flaw is the premise: `max_tokens` caps a single response, billing follows tokens actually generated, and `max_tokens` does not enter the output-tokens-per-minute rate limit. A real cost cap lives in your gateway, in workspace spend limits, and in the output contract.",
w:"A — there is no silent clamping; an over-ceiling value is rejected outright. B — `stop_reason: \"model_context_window_exceeded\"` describes overflow during generation, which is a 200 response with that stop reason, not a request-time 400. D — a missing `anthropic-version` header fails uniformly across models, not on one model only."},

{id:1595,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"A team ports a workload to a newer model and reuses the token counts they measured on the older model to project spend and context-window headroom. Why is the projection unreliable?",
o:["A. Token counts are identical across models, so the projection is fine and only the per-token prices need updating","B. Older models used a word-based tokenizer, so counts are not comparable in either direction","C. Requests above 200K tokens are priced differently on newer models, invalidating the arithmetic","D. Newer models use a newer tokenizer that produces roughly 30% more tokens for the same text — around 2.5 characters per token rather than around 4 — so both cost and window headroom must be re-baselined per model with `count_tokens` before any projection is trusted"],a:3,
e:"**Tokenization is model-specific, and a recent change moved it materially.** Newer models produce roughly 30% more tokens for identical text, which inflates input cost, output cost, and window consumption simultaneously — a prompt that comfortably fitted before can now overflow. `/v1/messages/count_tokens` is free and tokenizer-specific, so re-measure a representative sample against the target model. Transferable principle: **token counts are a property of the model, never of the text alone**, which is also why character-count heuristics fail.",
w:"A — counts differ by tokenizer, and a 30% shift is far too large to absorb in a price update. B — older models were also subword tokenizers; the difference is a newer subword vocabulary, not a word-based scheme. C — the full context window is billed at standard per-token rates on current models, so there is no volume-based rate tier to account for."},

{id:1596,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"A cost-reduction plan opens by moving every route to the cheapest tier and lowering `output_config.effort` everywhere. Nothing about caching, prompt hygiene, tool-surface size, or batch appears in it. What is the flaw in the plan's ordering?",
o:["A. The plan leads with quality trade-offs and skips the free wins. Prompt caching, input-token hygiene, tool-surface consolidation, tighter output contracts, and batch where latency allows all reduce spend without touching output quality, and should be exhausted and measured first — tier and effort genuinely trade quality for price and belong last","B. The ordering is correct: tier and effort are the largest levers, so they should always be applied first","C. The ordering is irrelevant because the levers are multiplicative and the end state is the same whichever order they are applied in","D. The plan should begin with a volume-discount negotiation, since engineering levers cannot match commercial terms"],a:0,
e:"**Sequence cost levers by whether they cost quality.** The free wins — caching a stable prefix, deleting unmeasured prompt bloat, consolidating tools, tightening the output contract, batching latency-tolerant work — change what you send and generate, not how well the model performs. Tier and effort change the model's behaviour and therefore require an eval to defend. Applying them first also destroys your baseline: once quality has moved you can no longer tell which change caused a regression. Measure a token profile from `usage` first, then work the free levers, then trade.",
w:"B — tier and effort are potent but they are the levers that put output quality at risk, and starting there forfeits savings that cost nothing. C — the levers interact rather than compose cleanly (caching is model-scoped, effort changes token volume), and ordering determines whether a quality regression is attributable at all. D — commercial terms may be worth pursuing but they are not an ordering principle for engineering work, and they do nothing about wasted tokens."},

{id:1597,d:"D2",s:"Cost & Token Management",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A workload caches a 40,000-token prefix. Traffic is bursty: some prefixes are read many times inside the TTL, while a large share are written once and never read again. How should the team reason about whether caching is paying?",
o:["A. Caching is free to attempt, so it can only help; a prefix that is never read simply costs nothing","B. A 5-minute cache write costs 1.25x base input and a read 0.1x, so an entry repays itself after a single read; a 1-hour write costs 2x and needs about two reads. A prefix written and never read is therefore a 25% surcharge on those tokens, which is why `cache_creation_input_tokens` staying high relative to `cache_read_input_tokens` is the signal that caching is losing money on that traffic","C. Cache writes are free and only reads are billed, so the write-once prefixes are harmless","D. Break-even is about 10 reads, because a read costs 0.1x of an uncached input token"],a:1,
e:"**Caching is an investment with a known break-even, and the `usage` fields tell you whether it cleared.** A 5-minute write at 1.25x is recovered by one read at 0.1x (1.25 + 0.1 = 1.35 against 2.0 uncached); a 1-hour write at 2x needs roughly two. So caching a prefix that is never read is a straight 25% penalty on those tokens. Monitor the ratio of `cache_creation_input_tokens` to `cache_read_input_tokens` per route: persistently write-heavy traffic should either be made cache-friendly (stable prefix, longer TTL) or not cached at all.",
w:"A — a write is billed at 1.25x base input, so an unread entry costs 25% more than not caching those tokens; caching is not free to attempt. C — writes are the more expensive operation of the two, at 1.25x for the 5-minute TTL and 2x for 1 hour. D — this inverts the arithmetic: the read multiplier is small, so break-even is one read, not ten; 10 reads would be the answer if reads cost the same as uncached input."}
);
